  "body": "Follow-up from the design note in [#4962](https://github.com/developing-today/code/pull/4962) (`doc/2026-11-03T14-55-00Z_design_id4630_oversized_replication_entries.md`), covering the one residual case the checkpoint-splitting fix ([#4966](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/4966)) does not reach for [#4630](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/4630).\n\n## Problem\n\n#4966 stops the writer from ever *producing* a `Checkpoint` entry over 192 KiB. But a journal compacted by an older binary can already hold one oversized `Checkpoint` on disk, and re-compaction is not automatic at open: `WorldCore::checkpoint_due` (`pkgs/id/src/world/core.rs`) requires `since_checkpoint >= checkpoint_every`, which is 0 after restore. So a restored legacy world with an oversized checkpoint keeps failing `split_entry_payload` forever — `send_snapshot` (`pkgs/id/src/world_replication_net.rs:206-243`) returns `PayloadTooLarge` before the first frame, with no indication of which entry or which bound was violated, and no path to recovery other than writing `checkpoint_every` new events.\n\n## Required change\n\nAt feed-build time (before `split_entry_payload`), detect an entry exceeding `MAX_REPLICATION_PAYLOAD_BYTES`:\n\n1. If the world has an active program and compaction is legal, re-compact immediately (a one-shot forced checkpoint), so the legacy oversized entry is replaced by bounded parts from #4966. Reuse the existing compaction path; do not invent a second writer.\n2. Otherwise (no active program, or compaction refused by the open-presence limit), return an actionable error naming the offending entry's ordinal, its encoded size, and the bound — instead of the bare `PayloadTooLarge` from the split step. The message must say that the journal needs compaction once the world can be checkpointed.\n\nDo not raise `MAX_REPLICATION_PAYLOAD_BYTES` and do not add continuation frames: both were rejected/deferred in the design note.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A synthetic legacy journal image containing one `Checkpoint` entry > 256 KiB, opened with an active program, is force-compacted at feed build and then replicates end-to-end (`split_entry_payload` → `send_snapshot` frames → `ReplicationTracker::accept`) with a matching cursor.\n- [ ] The same journal with no active program (compaction illegal) returns an error that names the entry ordinal, its size, and `MAX_REPLICATION_PAYLOAD_BYTES`, and is distinguishable from a malformed payload.\n- [ ] A journal with no oversized entry takes the unchanged fast path (no forced compaction, no extra serialization pass) — asserted by behavior, not timing.\n- [ ] `cargo test -p id --lib world::replication` and the `world_store` replication feed tests pass.\n- [ ] Module doc in `pkgs/id/src/world/replication.rs` states the legacy-journal path next to the per-entry guarantee.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Splitting checkpoints at write time (#4966).\n- Re-compacting journals that merely *want* it by policy — this is only the oversized-entry escape hatch.\n",
